**user**:
> What do you call the equations of motion used when you have a system of state variables? What relates the state variables over time?
**assistant**:
> They are usually called **state equations**, **equations of motion**, or a **state-transition system**.
> 
> For a state vector $\mathbf{x}_t$, the general discrete-time form is:
> 
> \[
> \mathbf{x}_t = \mathbf{F}(\mathbf{x}_{t-1}, \mathbf{u}_t, \boldsymbol{\theta}, \boldsymbol{\varepsilon}_t),
> \]
> 
> where $\mathbf{F}$ is the **state-transition function** (or **law of motion**), relating all state variables jointly across time.
> 
> In continuous time, this is typically written:
> 
> \[
> \dot{\mathbf{x}}(t) = \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}(t), \mathbf{u}(t), \boldsymbol{\theta}, t).
> \]
> 
> When several state variables affect one another, the collection of equations is a **coupled dynamical system**.
